00:05:17.820 --> 00:05:25.000
Pop the hood on speculative decoding and the draft model adds almost no extra memory because it stays in the same batch as the target.

00:05:25.580 --> 00:05:34.160
When the draft matches the target, the system accepts multiple tokens per verification step and cuts total latency by roughly half on typical workloads.

00:05:34.160 --> 00:05:39.620
When the draft diverges, the system discards the wrong tokens and falls back to standard

00:05:39.620 --> 00:05:44.540
autoregressive generation, so the worst case slowdown stays under 10%.

00:05:44.780 --> 00:05:48.720
The quality of the draft model matters most below 30 billion parameters.

00:05:49.320 --> 00:05:54.280
Above that size, the acceptance rate plateaus and extra draft compute stops paying off.

00:05:54.860 --> 00:05:59.480
Teams therefore choose a draft model one quarter the size of the target and accept rates

00:05:59.480 --> 00:06:03.060
between 60 and 80% as the practical operating range.

00:06:03.060 --> 00:06:12.000
Use speculative decoding when generation length exceeds 200 tokens and the draft model fits comfortably in the same accelerator memory as the target.

00:06:12.700 --> 00:06:16.020
Skip it for short prompts or when memory headroom is already tight.

00:06:16.860 --> 00:06:22.580
Before we go, the next round of M5 kernel releases should surface more tuned forks in the coming days.

